---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = "Why Your Funnels Aren't Converting (And It's Probably Not The Funnel)";
const description = "When a funnel underperforms, the page usually isn't the problem. Here's what to check after opt-in before you touch the design again.";
const url = 'https://systemsbygee.netlify.app/blog/why-your-funnels-arent-converting/';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: title,
  description: description,
  datePublished: '2026-09-14',
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Geethika Venkateshan', alternateName: 'Gee' },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Systems by Gee' },
  mainEntityOfPage: url
};
---
<Layout title={title} description={description} type="article" jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="tag tag--emerald">Automations</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 16px 0 14px;">Why your funnels aren't converting (and it's probably not the funnel)</h1>

      <div class="article-meta-row">
        <span class="avatar" style="background-image:url('/images/about.jpg')"></span>
        <span>By Gee</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Sep 14, 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
      </div>

      <div class="article-hero-img" style="background-image:url('/images/hero.jpg')"></div>

      <div class="toc">
        <h3>What's inside</h3>
        <a href="#followup">The follow-up that quietly never fires</a>
        <a href="#handoff">The handoff between automations nobody tested</a>
        <a href="#tags">A tag that didn't do what you assumed</a>
        <a href="#why">Why the page gets blamed anyway</a>
      </div>

      <p>You rebuilt the page. New headline, new testimonial, new button color. Same flat number staring back at you a week later.</p>
      <p>So you rebuild it again.</p>
      <p>Here's the thing nobody tells you before you spend another weekend on copy: most of the time, the page was never the problem. I've opened more "underperforming" funnels than I can count, and the leak is almost never in the part you can see. It's in what's supposed to happen the second someone opts in, the part that runs quietly in the background with no design to judge and nobody watching it work.</p>
      <p>You can polish a page for months and the number won't move, because the number was never about the page.</p>
      <p>Here's where I'd actually look first.</p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <h3>The quick version</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>A follow-up sequence that stopped firing months ago and nobody noticed.</li>
          <li>Two automations that were supposed to hand a lead to each other and never actually did.</li>
          <li>A tag you assumed was doing something it was quietly never set up to do.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2 id="followup">The follow-up that quietly never fires</h2>
      <p>A funnel isn't just the page. It's the page plus everything that's supposed to happen the moment someone says yes to it.</p>
      <p>Most of the time, that follow-up sequence was built once, early, and never looked at again. Since then you've renamed a pipeline, swapped an email platform, or edited the automation "real quick" and didn't retest the whole chain after.</p>
      <p>It doesn't send an error. It just stops. The opt-in still comes in, it still lands in your CRM, and the person on the other end waits for an email that was never coming.</p>
      <p>From where you're sitting, the funnel "isn't converting." From where the lead is sitting, they did their part and heard nothing back.</p>
      <p class="check"><strong>Check it this week:</strong> opt in to your own funnel with a real inbox you actually check. Time exactly what arrives, and when. If the honest answer is "not much," you've found the leak, and it isn't on the page.</p>

      <h2 id="handoff">The handoff between automations nobody tested</h2>
      <p>Most funnels don't run on one automation. They run on two or three, quietly handing a lead off to each other, one for the opt-in, one for the follow-up, maybe one more for booking a call.</p>
      <p>Each one usually works fine on its own. What almost never gets tested is the handoff between them, the moment automation one is supposed to trigger automation two.</p>
      <p>I see this constantly: a lead gets tagged correctly, the tag just doesn't trigger the workflow everyone assumes it triggers, because that connection was wired once, a while ago, and quietly stopped being true after something else changed.</p>
      <p>Nobody built this on purpose. It's just what happens when a funnel grows one addition at a time instead of getting looked at as one whole system.</p>
      <p class="check"><strong>Check it this week:</strong> pick one lead who opted in recently. Trace what actually happened to them, step by step, inside your CRM. Not what's supposed to happen. What actually did.</p>

      <h2 id="tags">A tag that didn't do what you assumed</h2>
      <p>Tags are supposed to be the quiet infrastructure holding a funnel together. They're also the easiest thing to get wrong, because a tag can exist, look correct, and still not be attached to anything.</p>
      <p>You added it thinking it would trigger a workflow, or exclude someone from an email, or route a lead to the right pipeline. Six months later, it's just sitting on a contact record doing nothing, and you have no way of knowing that from looking at the funnel's numbers.</p>
      <p>This is the one that's hardest to catch, because everything looks set up correctly. It just isn't actually doing the job you built it to do.</p>
      <p class="check"><strong>Check it this week:</strong> pick your three most important tags. For each one, find the automation that's supposed to react to it, and confirm it actually still does. Not that it exists. That it fires.</p>

      <h2 id="why">Why the page gets blamed anyway</h2>
      <p>Because the page is the part you can see.</p>
      <p>A follow-up that stopped firing, a handoff that never connected, a tag that's quietly inert, none of that shows up when you're looking at a landing page trying to figure out why the number is flat. So you do the thing that feels like progress: change the headline, swap the image, rewrite the copy again.</p>
      <p>Sometimes that helps a little. It's still treating a symptom while the actual leak keeps running underneath it, invisible, for another month.</p>

      <h2>The good news</h2>
      <p>None of this means starting over on the funnel. In almost every case I've found, the page itself was fine, or close to it. The fix wasn't a redesign. It was finding the one broken handoff and reconnecting it.</p>
      <p>The finding is the part that's hard to do from inside your own account, especially one you're already frustrated with. I do free audits exactly for this: fresh eyes on the whole chain, not just the page, a plain-English list of where it's actually leaking.</p>

      <div class="text-center" style="margin: 24px 0 8px;">
        <a href="#audit" class="btn btn--primary js-audit">Book a free audit</a>
        <p style="margin-top: 14px; color: var(--muted);">Or just DM me "audit" on Instagram or Threads. I read everything.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="share">
        <span>Share:</span>
        <a href={`https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=${url}`} target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Share on LinkedIn"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor"><path d="M4.98 3.5a2.5 2.5 0 11-.02 5.001A2.5 2.5 0 014.98 3.5zM3 9h4v12H3zM9 9h3.8v1.7h.05c.53-1 1.83-2.05 3.77-2.05C20.4 8.65 21 11 21 14.3V21h-4v-5.9c0-1.4 0-3.2-2-3.2s-2.3 1.5-2.3 3.1V21H9z"/></svg></a>
        <a href={`mailto:?subject=Worth a read&body=${url}`} aria-label="Share by email"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><rect x="3" y="5" width="18" height="14" rx="2"/><path d="M3 7l9 6 9-6"/></svg></a>
      </div>

      <div class="author-box">
        <span class="avatar-lg" style="background-image:url('/images/about.jpg')"></span>
        <div>
          <h3>Gee</h3>
          <p>GHL and systems strategist. I build the backend that runs your business for you, so you can lead instead of survive. Coaches, OBMs, and founders worldwide.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

</Layout>
